  "worked_solution": "A quadratic form in its canonical form shouldn't contain any cross-product terms (${x}_{i}{x}_{j}$ where $i\\ne j$). In terms of its matrix representation, this means that the corresponding matrix is diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- $P(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since it contains the cross-product term $2{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$\n\n- $Q(x)$ is in canonical form. Indeed, the form does not contain any cross-product terms (it contains only squares).\n\n- $R(x)$ is *not* in canonical form since its corresponding matrix is *not* diagonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$Q(x)$ only.\"",
